STEP 3: FIRST CLAUDE PROMPT (ANALYSIS ONLY)

Use enterprise-spring-payment-skill.

Analyze attached feature files.

Do NOT code.

Explain: 1. Business understanding 2. Existing flow understanding 3.
Impacted modules/classes 4. API impact 5. DB/SQL impact 6. Risks and
edge cases 7. Assumptions being made 8. Step-by-step implementation plan

Mention any ambiguity before coding.

STEP 4: ARCHITECTURE REVIEW

Example correction: paymentId is not unique. Settlement status comes
from downstream table. Repository pattern already exists.

STEP 5: LOW-LEVEL DESIGN PROMPT

Now generate low-level implementation design.

Show: - impacted classes - new DTOs - repository changes - service
flow - query approach - validation strategy - exception handling -
sequence of implementation

No coding yet.

STEP 6: IMPLEMENT IN SMALL SLICES

Repository: Implement repository layer using QueryDSL. Follow existing
project patterns. Do not touch service/controller.

Service: Implement service layer. Include validation and business rules.
Reuse existing utilities.

Controller: Implement controller layer. Maintain backward compatibility.
Follow API contract exactly.

Mapper: Implement MapStruct mapper.

Kafka: Implement kafka publishing logic. Ensure idempotency.

STEP 7: TEST GENERATION

Generate comprehensive test cases.

Include: - happy path - validation failure - null handling - duplicate
records - DB edge cases - API edge cases - performance considerations

STEP 8: PRINCIPAL ENGINEER REVIEW

Review implementation like a principal engineer.

Find: - bugs - performance issues - Oracle SQL concerns - thread safety
issues - backward compatibility risk - overengineering - missing test
coverage

